WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.173
It starts with ovulation: the release of an egg, or ovum, from an ovary. The egg moves into the oviduct, the tube that leads to the uterus.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:32.065
Sperm and egg are gametes, or sex cells. Chromosomes are threads of DNA in the nucleus. They carry the genes. Each gamete is haploid: its nucleus has one set of chromosomes, 23. Fertilisation is when the sperm nucleus fuses with the egg nucleus. This makes one cell, the zygote. It is diploid: two sets, 46.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:46.171
Step one: name the event first. Ovulation releases the egg. Fertilisation fuses the gametes. Then mitosis, a kind of cell division, makes the zygote into many cells: the embryo.

00:00:49.050 --> 00:01:04.325
Step two: describe mitosis in order. First, the DNA in each chromosome is copied. Then the chromosomes line up across the middle of the cell. The two copies of each are pulled to opposite ends. Then the cell splits in two.

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:20.789
This repeats again and again: one cell makes two, two make four, and so on. All the cells are genetically identical, with the same genes. By day five, the embryo is a hollow ball of about 100 cells, called a blastocyst.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:40.881
Identical twins form when one zygote or early embryo splits into two. Each part grows into a baby. Both came from the same egg and the same sperm, so they have the same genes. Two eggs fertilised by two sperm give non-identical twins.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:59.451
Now a real exam question. Read lines two to four of the passage. The zygote becomes a blastocyst, a hollow ball of about 100 genetically identical cells. Part b asks you to describe the process that forms a blastocyst. Four marks.

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:08.626
Identical cells come from mitosis. So name mitosis first. Then describe each division in order, one mark for each step.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:22.811
First, the DNA in each chromosome is copied. Then the chromosomes line up across the middle. The copies are pulled to opposite ends. Then the cell divides. This repeats until the blastocyst forms.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:28.925
Here is the full answer. This answer is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:43.917
A common mistake is to say fertilisation makes an embryo, or that the zygote is haploid. It makes a zygote, and the zygote is diploid. Also, the blastocyst forms by mitosis, not meiosis.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:59.348
Also, identical twins do not come from two eggs. Naming the four phases in order, prophase, metaphase, anaphase and then telophase, may earn one mark. But a list alone is not enough: say what the chromosomes do.

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:20.375
Your turn. In a clinic, a zygote divides until it has eight cells. One cell is removed and tested for a genetic disorder. How many chromosomes are in each of the eight cells? Name the process that made them. Explain why the test result also applies to the other seven cells. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:32.081
The zygote is diploid, with 46 chromosomes. Mitosis keeps the number the same, so each of the eight cells has 46. The process is mitosis.

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:46.453
For part c: mitosis copies the DNA exactly, so all eight cells are genetically identical. They all came from the same zygote. So the genes in the tested cell are the same as in the other seven.

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:58.357
So, every time: name the event, say the nuclei fuse to make a diploid zygote, and describe mitosis step by step. For identical twins, one embryo splits.
